-## Measured on an M3 Ultra (macOS 27.2), 4-bit, drafts off
+## Measured on an M5 Ultra (macOS 27.2), 4-bit, drafts off
 
-Through the served API with `tools/bench_openai.py`, 64 tokens, two repetitions, temperature 0:
+Cold prefill, one fresh process or server per arm, chunk 2048 (the M5 default), 2 repetitions that agree to 0.3%.
+Prompts are the first N tokens of `docs/recipes/*.md` in name order. Through the served API with
+`--prompt-cache-gib 0`, `max_tokens 1`, counting only requests that report `cached_tokens 0`:
+
+| prompt tokens | looped | 56 executions, no window | plain 32-layer | stock Qwen3.5-9B | mlx-lm 0.32.2, stock |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| 512 | **2710** | 2789 | 4815 | 4824 | 4721 |
+| 2048 | **2754** | 2803 | 4837 | 4841 | 4987 |
+| 8192 | **2718** | 2769 | 4850 | 4856 | 4846 |
+| 32768 | **2460** | 2505 | 4426 | 4429 | 4305 |
+
+The controls come from `tf-qwen27-run --max-tokens 0` on the same binary; the looped column matches it within 0.5%.
+Stock is `mlx-community/Qwen3.5-9B-4bit` @ `8b2b98c0`. Decode and short-prompt latency through `tools/bench_openai.py`,
+64 tokens, 2 repetitions, temperature 0:
 
 | metric | looped | plain 32-layer control |
 | --- | --- | --- |
-| decode | **33.19 tok/s** | **123.7 tok/s** |
-| prefill | 150.2 tok/s | 1457.8 tok/s |
-| TTFT | 0.032 s | 0.010 s |
+| decode | **59.96 tok/s** | 123.7 tok/s |
+| TTFT, 14-token prompt | 0.019 s | 0.010 s |
 
-The plain control is the same binary and the same weights with `loop_settings.counts=[1]` and no `mhc`, so it
-isolates the loop and the mHC. Keeping 56 executions but removing only the window gives **83.1 tok/s**, so the
-loop's own cost is as expected — and the window was the whole gap.
+`bench_openai.py`'s `prefill_tps` is the prompt's tokens over TTFT. With a 14-token prompt that measures one pass's
+latency, not prefill throughput: an earlier "150 tok/s" was 14 tokens over 0.093 s.
 
-The window was one kernel: `tf_mhc_branch`, the router. Skipping each mHC kernel in turn moved decode only for the
-router (11.3 -> 80.4 tok/s); `merge`, `enter` and `leave` were free. It dispatched **32 threads a row** and computed
-its eight outputs one after another, so each lane walked 128 elements per output per stream in a single simdgroup.
-An output a simdgroup — the same 32-lane order, run side by side — is **bit-identical** and takes decode from
-**11.3 to 35.3 tok/s** (33.19 through the served API). A wider 256-thread tree reduction reaches 42.4 but changes
-the fp32 reduction order and costs parity against the reference, so it is not landed.
+Prefill sits at 97-98% of the no-window column, and that column is the plain path times 32/56: each execution costs
+what a plain layer does and the GEMMs run at mlx-lm's rate. What is left of the window at 8k is about 50 ms of 3.0 s,
+mostly the router. Dense projections are 83% of GPU time at about 80 TFLOPS.
 
-Remaining: 33 against the 83 no-window figure. The router is one threadgroup at decode (one row), so it cannot fill
-the GPU.
+The window was one kernel, `tf_mhc_branch`, the router; `merge`, `enter` and `leave` were nearly free. Three changes,
+each bit-identical, so no FP64 study was needed:
 
-`tools/router_fp64.py` settles which reduction orders are open to it. Over **528 real rows** (three prompts,
-physical layers 4, 5 and 27, both sites, both visits) against an FP64 reference:
+| change | decode | prefill 8k |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| the router, one simdgroup, outputs in turn | 11.3 | — |
+| outputs side by side, one simdgroup an output | 36.5 | 2596 |
+| two streams fixed at compile time, lane loops unrolled by 8 | 62.7 | 2694 |
+| `merge` with two streams fixed at compile time | 63.6 | 2720 |
 
-| order | logit max\|err\| | logit mean\|err\| | pre max | post max | mix max |
-| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
-| 32-lane, an output a simdgroup (shipped) | 2.47e-05 | 1.48e-06 | 4.8e-07 | 1.18e-06 | 1.13e-07 |
-| split-K, 8 slices combined in order | 2.40e-05 | 1.48e-06 | 4.7e-07 | 1.21e-06 | 1.07e-07 |
-| split-K, 4 slices combined in order | 2.47e-05 | 1.48e-06 | 4.4e-07 | 1.21e-06 | 1.05e-07 |
+Each lane keeps its own summation order, so the router reads the same bits; unrolling only lets eight steps' loads
+overlap, and the fixed stream count is what lets the compiler do it. Prompt hashes at 512, 1003, 2k, 8k and 32k, the
+decoded tokens and the served token hashes equal the previous kernel's.
 
-All three sit at fp32's own rounding and differ by at most a few percent, so a reduction-order change is numerically
-free here — which is what CONTRIBUTING asks to establish before a reorder lands. It buys nothing, though: the
-8-slice split-K kernel measured **34.1 tok/s** against the shipped 35.3, because at one row the inner loop over the
-eight outputs costs more than the extra parallelism pays. So the 32-lane order stays, and the router's cost at one
-row is a property of the shape rather than of the arithmetic.
+Measured and not landed, all bit-identical: a router taking 4 or 8 rows a threadgroup to share the weight reads
+(+1% and -2%), and a simdgroup owning whole rows and all eight outputs (-5% before unrolling, within noise after). The
+router is latency-bound, not bandwidth-bound. A 256-thread tree reduction reaches 42.4 tok/s decode on the old
+kernel but changes the reduction order; `tools/router_fp64.py` shows such orders are numerically free (528 real rows
+against FP64, all at fp32 rounding).
+
+Remaining: decode is 60 against the no-window 83. At one row the router is a single threadgroup.
 
